# Git workflow

This repo rule overrides the default “only commit when asked” habit. Follow it on every coding turn.

## Commits

After a coherent unit of work is finished, create a git commit on the **current branch**. Do not wait to be asked. Do not switch or create branches unless the user asks.

Use the standard git safety protocol:

- `git status`, `git diff`, and `git log` before committing
- Stage only the files that belong to this change
- Pass the message through a HEREDOC using Conventional Commits: `type(optional-scope): summary` (for example `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`), then a short body on **why** when it is not obvious
- Never update git config, force-push, skip hooks, or commit secrets
- Never amend unless the user asked, HEAD is ours, and the commit has not been pushed
- If a hook rejects the commit, fix it and make a **new** commit — do not amend

Skip the commit only when there is nothing to commit, or the change is still mid-edit.

## Pull requests

When the work on this branch is ready for review (feature or fix complete, not a half-done WIP), **recommend** opening a GitHub pull request. Do not run `gh pr create` until the user agrees.

If the current branch is `main` or `master`, still commit here, and when recommending a PR suggest moving the work onto a feature branch first.
